Grow the command buffer of Tcl_AssembleCmd within an int (docs/M1-REPORT.md,
section 7.4; the same fix as patches/0005-sizes.patch).

Tcl_AssembleCmd, which the REPL and "source" of a large file use to
collect a command line by line, worked out its sizes in int: past 16384
bytes "bufSize*2" overflowed and bufSize was stored negative, so the
buffer was reallocated for every later line; and a command of more than
INT_MAX bytes overflowed totalLength, which could skip the growth and
copy the line past the end of the buffer. The sizes are now worked out
in long by TclGrowSize, which keeps them within INT_MAX and panics
("tcl16: string or list too long") when one command needs more.

diff -ru a/tclAssem.c b/tclAssem.c
--- a/tclAssem.c
+++ b/tclAssem.c
@@ -140,7 +140,7 @@
 				 * matched or not. */
 {
     register CmdBuf *cbPtr = (CmdBuf *) buffer;
-    int length, totalLength, c;
+    int length, c;
 
     /*
      * If an empty string is passed in, just pretend the current
@@ -158,18 +158,19 @@
      * Add the new information to the buffer.  If the current buffer
      * isn't large enough, grow it by at least a factor of two, or
      * enough to hold the new text.
+     *
+     * tcl16: the sizes are worked out in long by TclGrowSize, which
+     * panics if the command needs more than INT_MAX bytes; callers that
+     * can report an error instead check the length first (src/source.c).
      */
 
     length = strlen(string);
-    totalLength = cbPtr->bytesUsed + length + 1;
-    if (totalLength > cbPtr->bufSize) {
-	unsigned int newSize;
+    if ((long) cbPtr->bytesUsed + length + 1 > cbPtr->bufSize) {
+	int newSize;
 	char *newBuf;
 
-	newSize = cbPtr->bufSize*2;
-	if (newSize < totalLength) {
-	    newSize = totalLength;
-	}
+	newSize = TclGrowSize((long) cbPtr->bytesUsed + length + 1,
+		2L*cbPtr->bufSize);
 	newBuf = (char *) ckalloc(newSize);
 	strcpy(newBuf, cbPtr->buffer);
 	ckfree(cbPtr->buffer);
